WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.557
Temporal preparation remains important in Spark.

00:00:03.557 --> 00:00:07.447
Parse raw strings into typed dates or timestamps.

00:00:07.447 --> 00:00:12.252
Use Spark SQL date functions appropriate to that environment.

00:00:12.252 --> 00:00:14.732
And always state the output grain.

00:00:14.732 --> 00:00:19.422
For example, one row per event is different from one row per month.

00:00:19.422 --> 00:00:28.182
Many relational ideas transfer directly to Spark SQL: NULL semantics, date arithmetic, CTEs, joins, grouping,

00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:28.987
and windows.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:35.577
Syntax details may differ, but the same reasoning about grain, missingness, and cardinality still applies.

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:42.960
Portability is easiest when the business rule is clear before dialect-specific tuning.
